#
# DEVS:Internet/name_resolution
#
# How names like "aminet.net" are turned into addresses.  Copy this into
# DEVS:Internet and edit it, or let the installer write it for you.
#
# One keyword per line.  '#' and ';' start a comment.  "keyword value" and
# "keyword=value" are both accepted.
#
# WITH DHCP YOU DO NOT NEED THIS FILE.  The name servers arrive with the
# lease.  It is worth having anyway for the "hostname" line, and as somewhere
# to put a name server you would rather use than the one your router hands
# out.
#

# nameserver -- who to ask.  Repeat the line for a second and third server;
# they are tried in the order they appear here.
#
# On a home network this is nearly always the router itself.  If you would
# rather not use it, these two are public and work from anywhere:
#
#     nameserver 9.9.9.9
#     nameserver 1.1.1.1
#
nameserver 192.168.1.1

# domain -- appended to a name that has no dots in it, so that "fileserver"
# is looked up as "fileserver.localdomain".
#
# LEAVE IT OUT ON DHCP.  The lease names the domain and the resolver uses it;
# a domain written here is tried FIRST and the lease's after it, so a made-up
# name like "localdomain" costs a wasted query on every short name.
#
#domain localdomain

# search -- several domains to try, in order, instead of the single "domain"
# above.  One line, separated by spaces.  Anything the lease supplies is tried
# after these, not instead of them.
#
#search localdomain example.com

# hostname -- what this Amiga calls itself.  Letters, digits and hyphens.
#
# ENV:HOSTNAME is read too, and wins if it is set.
#
hostname amiga

# prefer -- whether a DHCP lease is allowed to replace the servers listed
# here (dynamic, the default) or not (static).
#
#prefer dynamic
